---
import '../../styles/development-editorial.css';
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import LegacyHeader from "../../components/LegacyHeader.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../../components/Hero.astro";
import ButtonPrimary from "../../components/ButtonPrimary.astro";
import ButtonSecondary from "../../components/ButtonSecondary.astro";
import DevelopmentStackJobs from "../../components/DevelopmentStackJobs.astro";
const canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/development/react/";
const proof=[
  {
    "title": "Interface, not the whole site",
    "text": "React handles the interface. Routing, rendering, data access and publishing each still need a tool and an owner."
  },
  {
    "title": "States are in the scope",
    "text": "We map validation, loading and failure states, then test whether a user can recover without starting again."
  },
  {
    "title": "Public routes still checked",
    "text": "We check direct URL visits, refreshes, metadata and links as well as in-app navigation, because a crawler arrives cold."
  }
];
const checks=[
  {
    "title": "Crawlable pages",
    "text": "Search engines need clean URLs, internal links, server-rendered content where needed and sensible indexation controls."
  },
  {
    "title": "Content structure",
    "text": "Pages need headings, body copy, proof, FAQs and conversion points that support SEO rather than sit behind visual components."
  },
  {
    "title": "Performance and Core Web Vitals",
    "text": "We reduce bloat, unnecessary scripts, layout shifts and render delays that weaken user experience."
  },
  {
    "title": "Redirect and migration safety",
    "text": "When replacing an old site, URL mapping and launch QA matter more than a prettier homepage."
  },
  {
    "title": "Schema and metadata",
    "text": "Titles, descriptions, canonical tags, structured data and Open Graph basics should be built in, not bolted on later."
  },
  {
    "title": "Analytics and forms",
    "text": "A site that cannot track enquiries properly makes SEO reporting weaker than it needs to be."
  }
];
const process=[
  {
    "title": "Audit",
    "text": "Review the current site, rankings, crawl behaviour, content, analytics and conversion risks."
  },
  {
    "title": "Plan",
    "text": "Map templates, URLs, content sections, redirects, schema and development priorities."
  },
  {
    "title": "Build",
    "text": "Develop the site around speed, accessibility, crawlability and editorial control."
  },
  {
    "title": "QA",
    "text": "Test pages, metadata, forms, mobile layouts, redirects, schema and indexation settings."
  },
  {
    "title": "Launch",
    "text": "Deploy with a launch checklist, Search Console monitoring and post-launch crawl checks."
  },
  {
    "title": "Improve",
    "text": "Use ranking, crawl and conversion data to refine pages after launch."
  }
];
const faqs=[
  {
    "question": "Is React a framework or a client-only way to build a site?",
    "answer": "React is a UI library. Routing, data loading, rendering and deployment depend on the tools around it. A React framework can generate static HTML or render on a server; a browser-only setup is another choice, not a requirement of React. For any page that must rank, that choice matters more than React itself."
  },
  {
    "question": "What separates React interface work from a full application build?",
    "answer": "The backend. An interface can use an existing API and authentication service. Building or changing the backend, database, payment processing or permissions is a separate scoping decision. Before agreeing the interface work, we need the API contract, test access and a named owner for those services."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should an interactive tool be tested against?",
    "answer": "We agree the normal, loading, empty, error and successful states, then check keyboard operation, labels, validation messages and recovery after a failed request. For a calculator, the business must approve the calculation rules: a control that works does not establish that its answer is commercially correct."
  },
  {
    "question": "How will our team edit content and publish changes?",
    "answer": "React does not supply a CMS. Content may come from an existing CMS or live in the repository. The publishing workflow should state what editors can change, what needs a code release and who can deploy. We check this before recommending any new tool, since an interface nobody can update is a cost that returns every month."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can you improve our current React site without replacing it?",
    "answer": "Yes, where a focused repair meets the requirement. We review routes, delivered content, metadata, internal links and the slow or unreliable interactions first. Your developer can implement from our SEO direction and QA findings. A switch to Next.js or another framework needs a reason beyond the React label."
  },
  {
    "question": "Who maintains the interface after handover?",
    "answer": "The proposal should name the owner of React and package dependencies, API changes, deployment access and regression tests. Hosting and backend support are not implied by an interface build. Better rendering can remove a technical obstacle, but it does not guarantee search rankings."
  }
];
---
<MainLayout title="React Development Philippines | Bright Forge" description="React development from the Philippines for calculators, dashboards and account views, with public routes rendered so search engines receive real pages." canonical={canonical} type="service" breadcrumbs={[{name:"Home",url:"https://brightforge.com.ph/"},{name:"Development",url:"https://brightforge.com.ph/development/"},{name:"React",url:canonical}]} faqs={faqs}>
<LegacyHeader slot="header" />
<Hero title="React <span>Development</span>" breadcrumbCurrent="React">
<Fragment slot="story"><p class="hero-subtitle">React development for the parts of a website that people use as well as read.</p><p>A calculator, account view or dashboard needs reliable data and controls people understand. React builds that interface well. It does not decide how a page reaches a search engine, and that gap is where React projects most often lose visibility. We scope the interface, its connection to your existing systems and the public pages that still need to be found in search.</p><div class="hero-proof">{proof.map((item)=>(<article><strong>{item.title}</strong><span>{item.text}</span></article>))}</div></Fragment>
<Fragment slot="actions"><ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Request a Site Review" /><ButtonSecondary href="#checks" text="See What We Check" /></Fragment>
</Hero>
<main class="dev-page">
<section id="checks"><div class="page-container two-column"><div><p class="eyebrow">Build focus</p><h2>React and SEO: define <span>the interface, its dependencies</span> and how each public page is delivered</h2><p>React handles the interface. A complete website also needs routing, rendering, data access and a publishing workflow. We check which of those your current platform already provides before proposing a framework or a replacement.</p><p>The SEO trade-off is specific. A browser-only React app sends every visitor the same near-empty HTML document and assembles the page with JavaScript. Google can render JavaScript, but as a second step it may defer, and link previews and many other crawlers read only the first response. Each route also needs its own title and description, a direct visit has to work without the app already loaded, and a missing page should return a 404 status. A client-only setup provides none of that by default. Rendering public routes on a server or at build time does.</p><p>Illustrative interface brief: a quote calculator should keep entered values after a validation error, explain missing inputs and offer a usable next step when the API is unavailable. The client supplies approved pricing rules; the build scope defines the controls and states to test. It shows the level of detail we write into a React scope.</p><p>Our own React build is <a href="/development/#workbench">Shinobi</a>, our brand site for creative businesses. It runs on TanStack Start with React 19, chosen because the site needed React components and route-led pages without becoming a one-page app. As our own brand, it is open to inspection route by route, alongside its published launch sheet.</p></div><aside class="panel"><h3>What we check first</h3><p>The user task, available API, authentication boundary and required interface states. For public routes, we also inspect delivered content, metadata and links before deciding whether rendering needs to change.</p></aside></div></section>
<DevelopmentStackJobs
  title="Four parts of a <span>React</span> build we scope separately"
  intro="The useful distinction is between a bounded interface and responsibility for the whole application. Each part below has its own owner and its own test."
  jobs={[
    {title:"Interaction and state", text:"We map the steps, validation, loading and failure states, and check keyboard access and whether a user can recover from an error without starting again."},
    {title:"API and backend boundary", text:"We identify which service supplies each value, who owns permissions and how errors reach the interface. New backend work needs its own scope."},
    {title:"Public route delivery", text:"We choose rendering through the host framework or build setup and check direct URL visits, refreshes, status codes, metadata and links as well as in-app navigation."},
    {title:"Release and maintenance", text:"We document publishing, package dependencies and deployment access, and agree which user flows must still pass whenever the API or the interface changes."}
  ]}
/>
<section><div class="page-container two-column"><div><p class="eyebrow">Who this suits</p><h2>Where <span>React is the right tool</span>, and what it leaves you to maintain</h2><p>React suits interactive tools, portals, dashboards, calculators and application interfaces, where the visitor does something more than read. The maintenance cost sits in the assembly. React is one dependency; the router, data layer, build tool and host are separate choices, and each needs someone to update and retest it. For a brochure site with a single calculator, one interactive component on an otherwise static page is often the cheaper answer.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>What your team supplies</h3><p>You bring the user journeys, approved business rules, design requirements and API documentation, and name the backend and release owners. We can then separate interface changes from new integrations, server work and ongoing support in the proposal.</p></div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container"><p class="eyebrow">Related development services</p><h2>Compare React with <span>the frameworks that deliver its pages</span></h2><div class="link-grid"><a href="/development/astro/"><span>Astro development</span>Static pages with React components only where a control needs them.</a><a href="/development/wordpress/"><span>WordPress development</span>An editor-led CMS, with interactive tools added as blocks or embeds.</a><a href="/development/nextjs/"><span>Next.js development</span>A React framework with server rendering and routing included.</a></div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container faq-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p><h2><span>React development</span> questions on scope, testing and ownership</h2></div><div class="faq-list">{faqs.map((faq)=>(<details class="faq-item"><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>))}</div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container"><div class="cta-card"><h2>Get a <span>React build scope</span> with the boundaries written down</h2><p>Send the user journey, the API you already have and the pages that must rank. We will review whether React is the right fit and where server rendering, static content or a simpler stack would be safer.</p><div class="cta-actions"><ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Request a Site Review" /><ButtonSecondary href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/" text="Technical SEO" /></div></div></div></section>
</main><Footer slot="footer"/>
</MainLayout>

